```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

class User 
{
    private ?int $id = null;
    private string $name = '';
    private string $email = '';
    private string $password = '';

    public function __construct(?int $id = null, string $name = '', string $email = '', string $password = '') 
    {
        $this->id = $id;
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->email = $email;
        $this->password = $password;
    }

    // Getters y Setters
    public function getId(): ?int { return $this->id; }
    public function setId(?int $id): void { $this->id = $id; }

    public function getName(): string { return $this->name; }
    public function setName(string $name): void { $this->name = $name; }

    public function getEmail(): string { return $this->email; }
    public function setEmail(string $email): void { $this->email = $email; }

    public function getPassword(): string { return $this->password; }
    public function setPassword(string $password): void { $this->password = $password; }

    /**
     * Valida que el email tenga formato correcto y la contraseña tenga al menos 8 caracteres.
     */
    public function validate(): bool 
    {
        if (filter_var($this->email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL) === false) {
            return false;
        }

        if (strlen($this->password) < 8) {
            return false;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

### 💡 Notas de uso:
- `filter_var($email, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)` es el estándar nativo de PHP para validación de emails.
- La clase usa tipado estricto (`declare(strict_types=1)`) y tipos explícitos para mayor seguridad.
- Puedes instanciar y validar así:
```php
$user = new User(1, 'Ana', 'ana@ejemplo.com', 'segura123');
var_dump($user->validate()); // true

$user->setEmail('invalido');
var_dump($user->validate()); // false

$user->setPassword('cort');
var_dump($user->validate()); // false (email válido pero password < 8)
```

